    "title": "Trump's Trade War Measures Overview",
    "text": "• President Trump imposed a 10% tariff on Chinese imports due to the country's role in fentanyl production.\n• Tariffs on Canada and Mexico, America's two largest trading partners, are prepared and could take effect in March after a 30-day suspension.\n• On March 8, 2019, Trump removed exemptions from his 2018 steel and aluminum tariffs.\n• Trump announced plans to sign sweeping reciprocal tariffs on U.S. trading partners, though the specifics of 'reciprocal' are unclear.\n• The proposed reciprocal tariffs aim to address unfair and discriminatory tariffs from both adversaries and allies.\n• These measures represent a shift from traditional trade policies, focusing on reciprocity rather than just bilateral or multilateral agreements.\n• Potential implications of these trade measures include more balanced trade agreements for American industries and the risk of retaliatory measures from trading partners.",
